## 1. One-line pitch

> Two players fight off the Forgetting to recover a memory they share, watching the world grow from 1-bit to 2.5D as it comes back.

## 2. How it ties to the theme "Time"

- Memories are time we've lost. Recovering them = recovering time.
- The graphics travel forward through gaming history (1-bit → 8-bit → 16-bit → 2.5D) as memories return. Time literally passes on screen.
- The Forgetting chases the players from behind like time running out.

## 3. Core concept

Two characters have lost a memory they share (e.g. two siblings, or a parent and child — the reveal of who they are to each other is the ending). The world starts as a faded, blurry, **1-bit** memory. As they defeat the Forgetting, the memory bar fills and the world **upgrades through graphical eras**, and each player **gets back an ability** they had forgotten.

## 4. Core gameplay loop

1. Players run right through an **endless, procedurally assembled side-scrolling platformer level** (Mario-style controls).
2. **Enemies** (creatures of the Forgetting: static, glitches, fog blobs) spawn in the level.
3. **Killing enemies drops memory fragments** → fills the **shared Memory Bar**.
4. A **Forgetting Wall** (wall of static/fog) advances from the left. If it touches a player, that player becomes "Faded" (see co-op revive). If both are Faded, the bar drops and the run is lost / restarts from the current era's start.
5. At **25% / 50% / 75%** on the bar → **era transition**: graphics upgrade + each player unlocks a new ability.
6. At **100%** → final memory restored → ending scene → (optional) endless score mode.

Target run length: **8–12 minutes** to reach 100% for a first-time pair.

## 5. Graphics eras

The world is ALWAYS built in a 3D scene with gameplay locked to one 2D plane (X/Y). Early eras just *look* 2D. This makes the final 2.5D switch cheap.

| Era | Bar range | Look | Camera | Audio |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1. **1-bit** (early childhood) | 0–25% | Black & white only, low-res pixelation, chunky blocks, no animation or 2-frame animation | Orthographic, side-on | Simple beeps, square wave |
| 2. **8-bit** (school years) | 25–50% | Limited colour palette (~16 colours), pixel sprites, simple tile backgrounds | Orthographic | Chiptune loop |
| 3. **16-bit** (teenage years) | 50–75% | Richer palette, more detailed sprites, **parallax** background layers | Orthographic | Fuller chiptune / SNES-style |
| 4. **2.5D** (adulthood) | 75–100% | 3D models or extruded blocks, real lighting, shadows, particles, bloom/post-processing | **Perspective**, slightly angled | Full soundtrack |

### Implementation rules for eras
- One global `EraManager` holds the current era (0–3) and fires an `on_era_changed(era)` event/signal.
- Every visual thing (tiles, players, enemies, background, UI) listens and swaps its sprite/material/model for that era. **Never** rebuild level layouts per era — only re-skin.
- 1-bit look can be done with a full-screen pixelation + 2-colour palette shader/post effect over simple art. Lower pixelation and widen palette for 8-bit and 16-bit.
- **Era transition moment:** freeze gameplay ~1.5s, screen flash or "boot-up" effect, short text like `MEMORY RESTORED: [name]` and `NEW ABILITY: [ability]`, then resume.
- **Final transition to 2.5D** is the showpiece: camera smoothly swings from orthographic side view to a slightly angled perspective view while flat blocks extrude into 3D. Spend polish time here.
- Placeholder art is fine until the art pass. Coloured cubes/rectangles with era-appropriate colours are acceptable for prototyping.

## 6. Players and co-op (this is how we win "best co-op")

- **Local co-op, 2 players, one shared screen.** No online multiplayer. No split screen.
- Input: Player 1 = keyboard WASD + Space (jump) + F (attack) + G (ability) **or** controller 1. Player 2 = arrow keys + Right Ctrl/Enter (jump) + Right Shift (attack) + / (ability) **or** controller 2. Make bindings easy to change.
- Must also work with **1 player** (P2 can join by pressing any of their buttons) so it can be tested alone, but the design is built for 2.

### Co-op mechanics
1. **Shared Memory Bar** — both players' kills fill one bar.
2. **Split abilities** — each player unlocks DIFFERENT abilities (table below). Some level chunks need both players' abilities to pass.
3. **Player-coloured memories** — some platforms/enemies are tinted in one player's colour and are only solid/visible/damageable for that player. Players must call out to each other. (P1 colour: warm, e.g. orange. P2 colour: cool, e.g. blue.)
4. **Faded & revive** — a player touched by the Forgetting Wall or killed becomes **Faded**: greyscale, ghostly, can still move slowly but can't attack or collect. The other player revives them by touching them (or standing next to them for 1s). Ties to "remembering someone".
5. **Both Faded = memory lost:** bar drops by 10% (never below current era's threshold) and both respawn together.
6. **Camera** follows the midpoint between players and keeps both on screen. If a player falls too far behind, they are pulled/teleported to their partner after 2s off-screen (with a small penalty, e.g. becoming Faded).

## 7. Abilities

| Era unlocked | Player 1 | Player 2 |
|---|---|---|
| Start (1-bit) | Run, jump, stomp | Run, jump, stomp |
| 8-bit | **Double jump** | **Dash** (short horizontal burst, damages enemies) |
| 16-bit | **Ground pound** (breaks cracked floors, kills enemies below) | **Wall jump** |
| 2.5D | **Glide** | **Time-slow pulse** (slows enemies and Forgetting Wall for 3s, 10s cooldown) |

- Each ability is a separate component/script that is **disabled until unlocked**. Unlocking = enable it + show the UI popup.
- Level chunks that require an ability must only appear once that ability is unlocked.

## 8. Enemies (the Forgetting)

Keep to **3 enemy types** max:
1. **Static Blob** — walks back and forth, dies to stomp or attack. Drops 1 fragment.
2. **Glitch Flyer** — floats in a sine wave, needs attack or dash/double jump to reach. Drops 2 fragments.
3. **Fog Brute** — bigger, takes 3 hits, appears from 8-bit era onward. Drops 5 fragments.

Optional if time allows: a **mini-boss at each era threshold**.

- Each enemy has an era-appropriate look (same swap system as everything else).
- Memory fragments are small pickups that drift toward the nearest player and add to the bar when collected.
- Spawn rate and enemy mix scale up slightly with era.

## 9. Memory Bar & Forgetting Wall

- **Memory Bar:** 0–100. Fragment = +1 by default (tune numbers in one config file). Small passive fill from distance travelled is optional; main source is kills.
- Era thresholds: 25 / 50 / 75 / 100.
- **Forgetting Wall:** a vertical wall of static/fog off the left side of the screen moving right at a steady speed that increases slightly each era. It stops players farming enemies. If the players are far ahead it catches up a bit faster (rubber-band) so it stays a threat.
- All tuning values (speeds, fragment values, spawn rates, thresholds) live in **one config/settings resource** so designers can tweak without touching code.

## 10. Endless level generation (chunk-based)

Do NOT write a full procedural generator. Use **hand-made chunks**:
- A chunk = a short section (~1–2 screens wide) of platforms, gaps, enemy spawn points and fragment spots, with a fixed entry height and exit height so chunks connect.
- **There is no level editor, so chunks are ASCII text files** in `/assets/chunks/`, loaded at startup. Anyone can make or edit a chunk in a text editor. Format: first line is a tag header, then a grid where each character is one tile:
  ```
  # abilities=double_jump min_era=1 difficulty=2 coop=false
  ..........................
  ..........F.......G.......
  ........#####.............
  ....S...........###...F...
  ####################..####
  ```
  Legend: `.` empty · `#` solid block · `=` cracked floor (ground pound) · `1` / `2` platform only solid for P1 / P2 · `S` Static Blob · `G` Glitch Flyer · `B` Fog Brute · `F` fragment · `|` wall (for wall jump). Add new symbols here if needed. Era numbers: 0 = 1-bit, 1 = 8-bit, 2 = 16-bit, 3 = 2.5D.
- `ChunkSpawner` keeps ~3 chunks ahead of the camera, picks the next one at random from the allowed pool, and deletes chunks once they're well behind the Forgetting Wall.
- Each chunk has tags: `required_abilities` (list), `min_era`, `difficulty`, `coop_required` (bool).
- Only pick chunks whose required abilities are unlocked. Right after an ability unlocks, force the next chunk to be one that uses it, so the player feels it immediately.
- Target: **10–15 chunks total**, including at least 4 co-op chunks (need both players' abilities, or player-coloured platforms).
- Chunk layouts stay the same across eras; they re-skin via the era system.

## 11. UI

- **Memory Bar** at top centre, with tick marks at 25/50/75. Bar style also upgrades per era (pixel bar → glossy bar).
- Small player indicators (P1/P2 colour, Faded state).
- Ability unlock popup.
- **Title screen:** game name, "Press any button to start", "P2 press to join".
- **Ending screen:** final memory scene (a short sequence of 3–5 still images or a simple animated scene in the 2.5D style) revealing who the two characters are to each other, then credits with team names.
- Pause menu with restart and quit.

## 12. Story

- No dialogue system needed. Story is told through: era popup text, short one-line memory names (e.g. "The first bike ride", "The treehouse", "The phone call", "Home"), and the ending scene.
- Writer/designer owns the exact text. Put all strings in one file.

